/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 8 — THE RENDERING WORKS
   Hell's industry. Register: CRONENBERG-INDUSTRIAL body horror — where L7
   was flesh-as-living-slum, L8 is flesh-as-FACTORY: bodies processed at
   scale, the damned rendered into product (tallow, candles, soap, the lamp-
   oil the not-children sang about up in the Shambles). The Gorgers (Tallow's
   kind) work the line. The body as raw material. Holds SIGIL-FRAGMENT 2.

   Hub = TheKillingFloor. Enter from L7 (TheChute → TheIntake, a one-way drop —
   you arrive as raw material; climb out via TheManway). Descent to L9 (TheRunoff)
   foreshadowed, not yet open. Depths in setup.depthOf (= 8).
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- ENTRY / HUB ---------- */

:: TheIntake [hell]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 3>>
The chute spits you out onto a sorting-grille above a drop, and for one bowel-loosening
moment you are exactly what the Works think you are: intake, raw material, a body
arriving at the top of a process designed to take bodies apart. Hooks pass overhead on
a clattering chain, most laden. A sorter the shape of a great gloved hand pauses above
you, weighs you with a touch that is almost clinical, and — finding you still moving,
still arguing, not yet stock — flicks you off the grille onto the floor with the mild
irritation of a worker who'll deal with you when you've stopped being a nuisance and
started being meat.

On your hands and knees on the floor you see what the chute spat you into the top of. The
grille runs the width of a hall too big to have an end, sorting the drop below it into
lanes — the still-warm one way, the cooled and stiffened another, a third lane for the part
that argues — and the whole arrangement has the unbothered tidiness of a fish-market at
dawn, scaled to swallow a city. Nothing here is angry at you. That is the part that gets
into the back of your throat: there is no cruelty in it anywhere, only sorting, only the
calm of a process that has never once had to hurry.

You get up fast. Everything here runs on the assumption that you are product that hasn't
been processed yet, and the only way to argue with that is to keep moving, keep being a
thing with somewhere to be, until you can find the way up and out.

<<link "Onto the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>


:: TheKillingFloor [hell]
<<nobr>>
The floor of the Works is a cathedral of disassembly: a vast hot hall under a roof lost
in steam, the air a solid wet wall of tallow-stink and scorched hair, and everywhere the
patient machinery of reduction — chains of hooks, banks of vats, the long line where
what comes in whole goes out as product.

Walk a little way onto it and the cathedral resolves into a shop floor. The far walls
draw back into haze rather than ending; the chains overhead sort themselves into circuits,
this one feeding the vats, that one hauling the line, a third returning empty for the next
load. Underfoot the stone runs to gutters, and the gutters run hot, and everything slopes,
gently, toward the same low places, so that even the floor is part of the process — built,
like the rest of it, to make sure nothing of value stays where it falls.

The Gorgers work it, fat pale tireless things that grew on discards and now process them,
and they do not look up as you pass, because a thing that walks with purpose reads, to them,
as staff, and a thing that walks as if it belongs is the one thing the Works are not
equipped to render.
<</nobr>>

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
You walk it like staff. That is the horror you carry out of this room: that the way to
survive the killing-floor is to move like you work here, and that you can, that some part
of you has learned the gait, and that the Gorgers' incurious acceptance of you feels less
like a trick you're playing and more like a homecoming.
<</if>>

<<link "Up a service climb, back toward the belly">><<goto "TheManway">><</link>>
<<link "Along the disassembly line">><<goto "TheLine">><</link>>
<<link "The foreman's office, up an iron stair">><<goto "TheTally">><</link>>
<<link "A cooling-room, where the tallow sets">><<goto "TheCooling">><</link>>
<<link "The boil-house, where the vats run">><<goto "TheBoilHouse">><</link>>
<<link "A runoff sluice, draining lower still">><<goto "TheRunoff">><</link>>


/* ---------- ASCENT / DESCENT ---------- */

:: TheManway [hell]
A manway climbs the wall of the Works — a service ladder of greasy rungs inside a shaft
meant for maintenance, not escape, threading up past the levels of the process toward the
warm wet organ-dark of the belly above. It is the way the staff come down and the way
nothing that is being processed ever goes. Climbing it, slick-handed, you pass viewing-
slots into the floors you're leaving, each a tableau of reduction, each a little further
along, until the red glow goes amber and the furnace-roar gives way to a wet, working
hush and the shaft narrows to a throat — and the throat is closed, capped from above by a
ring of pale muscle clenched across it like a fist. The underside of a sphincter. You have
come up beneath the belly's heart, and this is the far side of the gate that guards it.

From here it does not fight you. A sphincter is a one-way muscle: it holds against
everything trying to push //in//, and gives, helplessly, for anything leaving. You set your
shoulders to the slack pale ring and it relaxes around you with a long wet sigh, dilating
outward, and lets you up the way a belly lets up everything it has finished with — into
the knotted heart at the centre of all the grown flesh, and the cold incised thing set
into its scar.

<<link "Up through the slackening ring, into the belly's heart">>
  <<set $sphincterOpened to true>>
  <<goto "TheNavel">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back down to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>


:: TheRunoff [hell]
A sluice carries the Works' runoff out of the floor and down — the grease and the wash
and the unrenderable remainder, a slow hot river of tallow-scum sliding away into a dark
that glows, below, not furnace-red but a sick canary-yellow, and that breathes up a stink
the furnace can't account for: sulphur, brimstone, the stench of the actual bottom. The
Sulphur Deep. The runoff goes there because everything goes there; it is the drain of
hell, the place under the place, and the third fragment is somewhere in its yellow light.

A grille bars the sluice, but it has corroded to lace in the sulphur reek, and a person
determined and stupid enough could work through it and let the runoff carry them down the
last drop into the brimstone — into the Sulphur Deep, the bottom of everything, where the
third and last fragment waits. Everything an animal in you knows says do not go down there.
But there is only down there left, and the sigil is two-thirds a key, and you did not climb
through a rendering-works to stop one fragment short.

<<link "Work through the grille; let the runoff take you to the Deep">><<goto "TheBrimstoneShore">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>
<<link "Along a grease-choked trap">><<goto "TheGreaseTrap">><</link>>


/* ---------- GATE (guards the fragment) ---------- */

:: TheRenderLock [hell]
The fragment is in the heart of the process, in the master vat, and the way to it runs
between the plates of the great press — the render-lock, where the line's load is crushed
and the oils driven off, two house-sized faces of grooved iron that close, and pause, and
open, and close, on the relentless timing of the machine. To go through is to time the
pause and cross the bed of the press while it gapes. Mistime it and you become, very
quickly and very thoroughly, product.

<<if $lineStilled>>
But you stilled the line at the foreman's tally, and the press hangs open and dead, its
plates gaping on a stopped machine, the bed of it a safe dark road into the master vat.
You cross between the iron faces and they do not close.

<<link "Through the dead press, into the master vat">><<goto "TheTallowHeart">><</link>>
<<else>>
The press runs, and runs, and the pause is just long enough for a brave fool and just
short enough to kill a slow one. You can time it and cross the running bed — and trust
your legs against the machine — or go up to the foreman's office and find the way to
still the line.

<<set _tlbl to "Time the press and cross the running bed (" + setup.tollTag("legL", "filth", 24) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "legL" "filth" 24>>
  <<goto "TheTallowHeart">>
<</link>>
<<link "Up to the foreman's office, to still the line">><<goto "TheTally">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back to the foreman's office">><<goto "TheTally">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE SIGIL-FRAGMENT ---------- */

:: TheTallowHeart [hell]
The master vat is the size of a chapel and full to the brim with rendered fat, white and
still and faintly luminous, the distillate of more bodies than you will let yourself
estimate — and floating at its centre, held up by the very thing it refuses to dissolve
in, is the second fragment. A third of a sigil, cold incised bone, riding the tallow like
a splinter the whole vast process of reduction could not break down, because the mark is
older than rendering, older than hell's industry, and the Works have boiled around it for
an age and failed to take it in.

<<if $sigil.includes("sigil_rendering")>>
The vat closes white and smooth over the place you reached into it. The Works will go on
refining, and will go on failing to refine the one thing that was ever worth refining out
of all that reduced and rendered grief. You have it. Two.
<<else>>
<<link "Reach into the tallow and lift the second fragment free">>
  <<set $sigil.push("sigil_rendering")>>
  <<corrupt "armR" "filth" 4>>
  <<goto "TheTallowHeart">>
<</link>>
<</if>>

<<if $sigil.includes("sigil_rendering")>>
<<if setup.hasSigil()>>
And that is three. The Unbinding Sigil is whole — you can feel the pieces of it pulling
toward each other in your kit, hateful to everything around them, eager to be a door.
<<else>>
One more lies at the bottom of everything, in the Sulphur Deep, in the canary-yellow light
below the runoff. Two of three. The mark grows heavier and colder and more *certain* the
more of it you carry.
<</if>>

<</if>>
<<link "Back through the press">><<goto "TheRenderLock">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE FOREMAN'S OFFICE (gate key) ---------- */

:: TheTally [hell]
The foreman's office overlooks the floor through a wall of grease-filmed glass: a high
iron room of ledgers and levers, where a Gorger fatter and stiller than the rest keeps
the tally — the count of what comes in and what goes out, written in a neat dead hand in
books that go back further than the city you came from. The great lever-bank along one
wall runs the line, the press, the whole reciprocating process, and the foreman attends
it with the bored competence of a thing that has run this shift since before shifts.

The longer you stand in it the more the office gives up. The ledgers are honest
documents — columns ruled and balanced and carried forward, intake against output,
every soul that came down the chute entered and totted and reconciled to the last
unrenderable ounce. The glass films the floor below to a grey dumb-show, the screaming
gone out of it, the abattoir reduced to figures moving between columns, and you catch
your own eye running down the open page out of old habit, the way you would check a
receipt. That is the horror the office keeps: somewhere in a book like this, in the
same dead-neat hand, on a line not yet ruled, there waits a space the width of you —
your weight in, your tallow out, the remainder filed under waste — and the foreman will
enter it when the time comes without once looking up, because to the books you were
never a person to be wronged, only a figure they had not yet had occasion to carry forward.

<<if $lineStilled>>
The line is stilled at your hand, the master lever thrown, the floor below gone quiet and
the Gorgers standing about it confused, a shift interrupted for the first time in an age.
The foreman has not moved. It is, you think, waiting to see what you imagine happens next.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Read the tally; learn the lever that stops the press">>
<<set $lineStilled to true>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 2>>
You read enough of the dead neat hand to find it: the master lever, the one that stills
the line and gapes the press dead-open. You throw it. Below, the great machine sighs to a
stop, and the silence that follows is the loudest thing in hell, and the foreman turns its
patient head to look at you with an interest you would much rather it didn't have. You have
your road to the vat. You have also, the foreman's look makes clear, been *noticed* by the
management.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "To the render-lock, and the master vat">><<goto "TheRenderLock">><</link>>
<<link "Out onto the foreman's catwalk">><<goto "TheForemansWalk">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: TheCooling [hell]
A cooling-room, off the heat of the floor: rack on rack of set tallow, the product
resting, gone from boiling-white to a cold candle-cream, the air for once not wet and not
reeking but merely cold and faintly waxy, almost clean. Nothing renders here. Nothing
boils or screams or moves. The Gorgers don't come — there is no work in a room where the
work is already done — and so it is, of all the unlikely places, safe: a cold larder at
the heart of the abattoir, where you can put your back to a rack of the finished and be,
for a moment, not next.

You sit in the waxy cold and let the floor's heat sweat out of you and the ringing leave
your ears. The set tallow at your back holds the cold like stone. You try not to read the
labels on the racks, and you don't, and that small refusal is the most of yourself you
have managed to keep in hours.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<link "Through to the tallow-store">><<goto "TheTallowStore">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>


/* ---------- CACHES ---------- */

:: TheTallowStore [hell]
The tallow-store is the Works' treasury, of a kind: barrel on barrel of the rendered
product, graded and stamped and waiting for the long haul up to the Shambles markets, and
salted through the casks — dropped by haulers, shaken loose in the grading, pocketed and
lost across the long economy of selling people by the barrel — the cold coin the trade
runs on.

You walk the aisles between the casks the way you'd walk a bonded warehouse anywhere, and
that is the unbearable part of it: the order. Every barrel chalked with its grade and its
weight, the stacks squared off and chocked against rolling, a running-total slate by the
door kept current to the cask. Somewhere a draught moves through the stacked cold and the
whole store breathes out at you, faint and even and clean, the rendered hush of a thing
warehoused and waiting. It does not smell of dying. It smells of inventory.

<<if $tallowStoreLooted>>
You've been back for the loose coin between the casks. The casks themselves you leave, stamped
and waiting, and you do not read what they're stamped with, and you carry that small mercy
out with you.
<<else>>
The cold coin the trade runs on is salted through the casks — dropped by haulers, shaken loose
in the grading, pocketed and lost — waiting in the grease for a free hand and a reason to come
back for it.
<</if>>

<<link "Through to the candle-works">><<goto "TheCandleWorks">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the cooling-room">><<goto "TheCooling">><</link>>


:: TheCandleWorks [hell]
The candle-works, where the better grade of tallow becomes light: a long room of dipping-
racks and molds, ten thousand candles hung to set, pale and patient, each one a portion of
somebody rendered down to the part that burns. This is where the Shambles' lamps come from,
and the not-children's jingle, and the warm domestic glow of every hearth in hell. The
wicks, you notice and wish you hadn't, are hair. The coin is here because everything's coin
is here: tipped into the trade, fallen in the dipping, glinting in the trays of cooling
fat.

<<if $candleWorksLooted>>
You've gleaned the trays already. The candles you leave hanging, ten thousand patient lights
waiting to be lit in the homes of hell, and you take nothing of them but the knowing, which is
more than enough.
<<else>>
The coin is here because everything's coin is here: tipped into the trade, fallen in the
dipping, glinting in the trays of cooling fat — for whoever's willing to put an arm into what
they pour the candles from.
<</if>>

<<if not $dipperTalked>>
A dipper works a rack near the molds, lowering frames of wicks into the fat and lifting them set, and
does not look up — a tradesman at his trade, with the unbothered hands of a man who has done this a very
long time.
<<linkreplace "Ask the dipper about the work">><<nobr>><<set $dipperTalked to true>>
//Mind your sleeve on the rack — they're still soft.// He lifts a fresh row, pale and dripping. //Forty
year I dipped, up top. Good clean trade, candle-dipping; you'd not think it'd come to this. But it's the
//same trade,// see — that's the joke of it. Same dip, same set, same count. Only the stock changed, and
the stock was never my department.// He sets the frame to cool. //Wick's hair. Aye. You get so you don't
see it. That's the bit that gets you in the end — not the seeing. The //not.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The dipper has gone back into the count and will not be drawn out of it; he said his piece between two
rows, and the rows do not stop.
<</if>>

<<link "On, to a vat of soap">><<goto "TheSoapVat">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the tallow-store">><<goto "TheTallowStore">><</link>>


:: TheSoapVat [hell]
The soap-vat, the cleanest product the Works make and the foulest joke they tell: great
settling tanks of the stuff, marbled grey-green and faintly perfumed, pressed into bars
and stamped with a wholesome word, soap to wash with, soap to be *clean*, made of exactly
the people who came down here to find a way to stay themselves. The bitter coin of it is
caught in the skimmings and the spill.

<<if $soapVatLooted>>
You've skimmed the coin already. The bars cure in their racks, wholesome and waiting, and you
leave them to it, and you do not, ever, intend to wash with soap again.
<<else>>
The bitter coin is caught in the skimmings where the lye runs over — free to anyone who'll
reach into the slick for it, and trade a little squeamishness for it, which is itself, you
note, the trade working.
<</if>>

<<if not $soaperTalked>>
A skimmer works the lye-line, lather to the wrists, and turns to you with the proprietary warmth of a
man who wants you to know how important he is here.
<<linkreplace "Hear out the lather-man">><<nobr>><<set $soaperTalked to true>>
//You smell that?// He lifts a dripping double-handful, proud as a vintner. //That's //bergamot.// My
signature. They brought me //down// for this — there's not three noses in all of hell could grade a batch
like I grade it.// His hands, you notice, end at the lye-line, gone away to the wrists; the air off the
vat is the reek the whole room warns of. //Tell you what — friend's rate — drop a coin in the skimmings
and I'll cut you a bar of the //real// lather. Takes the works right back off a man. Clean as the day you
signed.// He cannot, of course. There is no such bar. He believes there is, which is the worse thing.
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The lather-man is back at his grand imaginary trade, grading a batch nobody ordered, with no more
attention for you than the foam has.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a grease-choked trap">><<goto "TheGreaseTrap">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the candle-works">><<goto "TheCandleWorks">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARES (bad ends) ---------- */

:: TheVat [hell]
A vat between fillings stands open beside the line, empty but for the hand's-depth of
last batch cooling in its bottom, and a ladder down its inner wall for the workers who
scrape it out, and a warmth coming off it that reaches you across the floor like a hand.

You step to the lip without quite deciding to. The inside is sheer and softly white,
scoured by ten thousand scrapings to a curve like the inside of an egg, and the warmth
rises out of it steady and close and bodily, the only warmth down here that isn't trying
to render you, or seeming not to. The ladder goes down into it, worn bright at every rung.
Easy. Inviting. A short climb to the bottom of a thing.

And then the smell finds you — and the smell is the trap, because the smell is not the
reek you'd expect but something deep and rich and almost *nourishing*, the smell of broth,
of rendering, of the warm fat heart of the process, and the tired starved animal of your
body leans toward the open vat and the warm rich dark at the bottom of it with a hunger
that has stopped being about food.

<<link "Climb down into the warm vat. Just to be warm.">><<goto "EndingRendered">><</link>>
<<link "Step back from the lip of the vat.">>
  <<soothe 5>>
  <<goto "TheBoilHouse">>
<</link>>


:: TheWard [hell]
By the hooks a Gorger has set up the way Tallow did, far above in the fatberg — a renderer
that does bespoke work, that doesn't process you against your will but *offers*, gently,
to render a piece of you into something that will keep the rest of you alive a little
longer down here.

It has made itself a little shop out of the line's leavings. A bench of bone and a row of
small clean tools laid out in order of use; a trough of warm rendered fat kept at the
blood-heat; a few finished pieces hung up to cure, hides and plates and hardened things
that were softer once, the way a cobbler hangs his samples. It is the only Gorger on the
whole floor that has bothered to make a place of its own, and the difference is precisely
the thing that frightens you about it: it is not processing the unwilling. It is taking
custom.

//Soft thing,// it says, not unkindly, paddling its hands in a trough. //Cold thing. Let me
take the part of you that bruises and give you back a hide that doesn't. A small trade.
You've so much you're not using.//

<<if $hasWard>>
You are already warded — already carrying a piece of yourself rendered to armor, from a
bargain made higher up — and the Gorger sees it, and nods, professional to professional,
and does not press. There is, apparently, a courtesy among the rendered.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Take the ward — let the Gorger render a piece of you hard">>
<<set $hasWard to true>>
<<corrupt "armR" "filth" 25>>
You give it the part that bruises and it gives you back a hide that doesn't, working
quickly and kindly, and you rise harder to kill and softer in some other place you can't
name, and the Gorger wipes its hands and says //good// the way you'd praise a thing for
holding still, and you understand you have just been, in the smallest and most consensual
way, *processed*.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the hooks">><<goto "TheHooks">><</link>>


/* ---------- COMBAT ---------- */

:: ThePits [hell]
Below the line the floor opens on the pits — where the intake that isn't ready for the
line is held, a sunken holding-pen of the raw and the unwilling, and where one of the
Gorgers has come down to cull the pen of anything that's stopped being worth the trouble of
processing.

The grating you stand on is the lid of it. Through the squares of iron the pen seethes
faintly in the dark below, a slow pale shifting of the held and the waiting, packed the way
livestock pack against a wall — not fighting, past fighting, only pressing instinctively away
from the low end where the culling happens. There is a smell up through the grate of the raw
material before the heat gets to it, which is worse, somehow, than the rendered: the smell of
the thing still entire, still a thing, an hour or a day from the line.

It is up to its elbows in the work when your weight on the grating turns its great pale head,
and it considers you, and decides — the way they all decide — that a thing walking loose on
the killing-floor is either staff or an error, and that it is, on balance, easier to render
an error than to check. Below it a grafted works the pen-floor, sorting the cull into piles
with more hands than the job needs — the pits keep an assembler the way the line keeps a
knife — and at the gorger's grunt it stops sorting and starts climbing.

<<if $pitGorgerCleared>>
The Gorger you put down lies among the intake it was culling, indistinguishable now, raw
material at last, its grafted sorter gone back to parts beside it. The pits are quiet. Whatever was held here that could climb has climbed;
what's left does not look up.

<<link "Back up to the line">><<goto "TheLine">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<scentof `["gorger","grafted"]`>>
<<link "Put the Gorger down before it sorts you">>
  <<run setup.startFight(["gorger", "grafted"], "ThePitsCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back up to the line, off the grating">><<goto "TheLine">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: ThePitsCleared [hell]
/* lands here after the gorger fight; sets the flag, then folds back */
<<set $pitGorgerCleared to true>>
The Gorger comes apart slower than a thing should, being mostly the same rendered stuff it
processes, and goes down into the pen it was culling and is, within a breath, just more of
it — the Works do not distinguish, in the end, between the staff and the stock. You climb
out of the pits with the cull's blood to your elbows too, and try not to think about how
little, now, that distinguishes you from either.

<<link "Back up to the line">><<goto "TheLine">><</link>>


/* ---------- PASSAGE / ATMOSPHERE FILLER ---------- */

:: TheLine [hell]
The disassembly line: the long spine of the Works, where what comes in whole is taken
methodically to pieces by a sequence of stations, each Gorger at its post performing its
one practised cut as the chain hauls the work past, the whole thing running with the awful
unhurried competence of any line anywhere, of any honest factory turning raw stuff into
goods.

The further down it you look the less is left. You can read the whole process off it like a
chart: entire at the head of the line, and then a station gone, and then another, each
Gorger taking only its own portion and passing the rest on without interest, so that a body
travels the chain shedding itself a cut at a time until what reaches the tail is barely an
outline of what arrived. The cuts are not savage. That is the thing the eye keeps insisting
on and the mind keeps refusing: they are *neat*, each one square and considered, a tradesman's
work, the same cut a thousand thousand times until it is no longer a cut at all but a habit
of the hands.

The only difference, and it is no difference at all, is the raw stuff. You walk beside the
chain the wrong way, against the direction of the process, which is the one direction the
Works are not built to handle, and it is the only thing keeping you off the hooks.

<<if not $lineWalkerHeard>>
One of the suspended, half onto a hook already, turns toward you as you pass against the chain — slow,
serene, in no distress, and seeming to want to tell you something while it still can.
<<linkreplace "One of the suspended turns toward you, and seems about to speak">><<nobr>><<set $lineWalkerHeard to true>>
//You're doing it right, you know — going the wrong way down the line. It's the only thing that keeps
them reading you as staff.// It turns, gently, on the slow hook. //I did it the longest time. You'd be
surprised how long. And then one shift my legs just — //agreed// with the chain. That's all it was. Not
a choice; I'd have sworn to my last breath I'd never //choose// it. Just a morning the walking-against
weighed more than the being-carried, and the place is so very patient, and the hook comes round on its
own.// A small, kind smile. //You won't feel the turn. Nobody does. You'll only notice you've stopped
walking //against,// and how much lighter that is.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 4>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
She has turned back the way the hook is turning her, serene, finished with you; the chain hauls her on a
little further than last time.
<</if>>

<<link "Down into the holding-pits">><<goto "ThePits">><</link>>
<<link "A bank of hooks, off the line">><<goto "TheHooks">><</link>>
<<link "A vat standing open, beside the line">><<goto "TheVat">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>


:: TheHooks [hell]
A holding-bank of hooks, where the work hangs waiting its turn on the line — a slow forest
of the suspended, turning gently in the heat-draught, patient, processed-elsewhere into
silence, waiting with the terrible passivity of meat for the chain to come and carry them
on.

The forest goes back further than the light. They hang in ranks, evenly spaced, each on its
own hook on its own length of chain, so that the whole bank turns together when a draught
moves through it, a slow synchronized rotation like a field of strange pale wheat. They do
not crowd. They do not reach. Whatever was done to them upstream was done well enough that
they have stopped wanting anything, and the stillness of that — a roomful of the patient,
none of them trying to leave — is heavier on you than any amount of screaming would be.

You move through them carefully, parting them like a bead curtain, and you do not look at
faces, because some of the hanging work still has them, and some of those are still, very
slowly, changing expression.

<<if not $hooksSpoke>>
One face among the hanging work is still changing expression — slowly, lucidly, in the gaps between the
chain's hauls — and as you part the bodies it finds you with its eyes, and works its mouth, wanting the
last of its words spent on something still walking.
<<linkreplace "Stop at the one face still changing">><<nobr>><<set $hooksSpoke to true>>
//Going down?// it manages. //Course you are. Listen — the rendered talk, on the hooks; nothing else to
do but talk — and they all say the same of the //last// piece. The one the vat couldn't boil down. It
went //further// — rode the runoff out the bottom of the Works and down into the yellow light, the
sulphur, the floor of everything.// The chain shudders; it has a moment more. //If you're hunting the
cold mark that walks a soul out the front — and I think you are — that's where the last of it waits. Down
the runoff. All the way down. Past where I'm going.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 2>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The chain has hauled it one station on; the face is gone now, smoothed to the terrible passivity of the
rest, and it cannot speak, and you would not want to hear what it would say if it could.
<</if>>

<<link "To a renderer that does bespoke work">><<goto "TheWard">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a fall of molten tallow">><<goto "TheTallowFalls">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the line">><<goto "TheLine">><</link>>


:: TheBoilHouse [hell]
<<roomart "boilhouse">>
<<if $pressedReturn>>
They haul you onto the boil-house floor and a Gorger shoves a long paddle into your hands without a
glance — no contract, no binding, just a body they caught idle and a vat that needs stirring. One
shift, and then off the floor with you.

<<linkreplace "Take the paddle they pressed on you and stir">>
<<set _ps to setup.doPressedShift()>>
You stir, and the white steam renders a little more <<= _ps.cls>> into whatever part of you stands
nearest the heat, a degree at a time, the way the works takes everything that lingers. Pressed work
pays no coin; it only takes. When the vat is turned the Gorger jerks its head at the door.

<<link "Back to it">><<set _to to $pressedReturn>><<set $pressedReturn to "">><<goto _to>><</link>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The boil-house, the furnace heart of the Works: ranks of vats over a fire that has not
gone out since fire was invented, each one rolling at a slow heavy boil, throwing up a fat
white steam that condenses on every surface and rains back down warm and greasy, so that
the whole room weeps tallow and breathes tallow and is, you realize, slowly rendering
*everything* in it, including the air, including you, a degree at a time. The Gorgers tend
the vats with long paddles, skimming, stirring, and do not mind the heat, being already
mostly the thing the heat is for.

<<if $workOwed and $workStation == "vats">>
You are signed to this floor. The binding walked you back here the moment the world last put
you down — fed, housed, *kept*, exactly as the contract promised — and a Gorger presses a long
paddle into your hands without once looking up, because you are not a visitor here, you are
another hand on the line. The doors off the floor will not open for you; you can feel the curse
holding them, patient as the boil, until you have taken your turn at the vat. A shift is owed,
and the works keeps its books in a heavier ink than coin.

<<linkreplace "Take up the paddle and work off the shift you owe">>
<<set _s to setup.doShift()>>
You stir. The boil is heavier than water and does not want the paddle back; it climbs the shaft
toward your hands in slow fat ropes, and the white steam renders into you the whole while you
stand over it — the works taking its wage the only way it knows, a degree at a time, settling its
grey aggregate into whatever part of you stands nearest the heat. When you set the paddle down
the debt, for this return, is paid: you feel the curse on the doors ease — until the next time the
world knocks you down and the binding reels you back to the boil, a little more of you left in it.
<<if _s and _s.leather>>

The works keeps its books both ways. A Gorger jerks its head at a rack by the door, where a
cured-leather apron and jerkin hang among the aprons — //long service//, as near as the floor comes
to saying it — and you understand the gear is now yours to take. Real armour, hard-worn, off the
boil. //(You've worked the vats <<= _s.shifts>> times — free leather armour issued.)//
<</if>>

<<link "Off the floor, toward a fall of molten tallow">><<goto "TheTallowFalls">><</link>>
<<link "Off the floor, back to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>
<<link "To a vat standing open, off the ranks">><<goto "TheVat">><</link>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
No one on the floor is //paid//, exactly — but the boil renders coin out of its load the way it
renders everything, and the dead came down with full pockets, and what the works drives up out of
them is anybody's who'll reach into the heat for it. The Gorgers don't grudge it. The Gorgers are
long past grudging anything. How far in you reach is the only question, and the works charges for
the reaching by the depth.

<<linkreplace "Take up a paddle and stir a while, skimming the bright things that surface">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(4, 7)>><<corrupt _seg "filth" _amt>>
<<set _pay to random(3, 6)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
You stir, because everyone on the floor stirs, and the warmth is the most nearly-human thing left
in hell — and you skim as you go, lifting the bright surfaced things the boil has driven out of its
load, <<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> coin<<if _got neq 1>>s<</if>> of old pocket-money rendered clean off its owners<<else>>old pocket-money rendered clean off its owners — that curdles to rubbish the instant it is yours<</if>>. The
white steam renders quietly into you the whole while, the works taking its small grey wage a degree
at a time, the way it takes everything that lingers near it.
<</linkreplace>>

<<linkreplace "Strip down and get in — scrub a cooling vat to the bottom, where the heavy coin settles">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(10, 15)>><<corrupt _seg "filth" _amt>>
<<set _pay to random(12, 18)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
You climb in. That is the whole of it, really — the one thing no one else on the floor will do,
which is exactly why it pays: down into a cooling vat to scrub it out from the inside, standing
chest-deep in the tallow and the grey settled remainder, and you come up with the heavy coin that
sinks past all skimming, <<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> of it,<<else>>which rots to rubbish in your fist,<</if>> the gold the dead kept deepest and carried furthest down.
But a vat does not need to be boiling to render you — chest-deep in what it leaves is more than
enough — and you climb out wearing a great cooling weight of the works that does not wipe off, and
does not, you come to understand, mean ever to.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "A vat standing open, off the ranks">><<goto "TheVat">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a fall of molten tallow">><<goto "TheTallowFalls">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the killing-floor">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>
<</if>>
<</if>>


:: TheGreaseTrap [hell]
A grease-trap, where the Works catch what even hell won't waste — a sunken stone tank
choked with the cooled and congealed unrenderable, the scum and the float, a grey-white
geology of the parts the process couldn't drive an oil out of.

You crouch at the rim and the geology resolves. It is bones, mostly, but not only — there
are buttons in it, and teeth, and the small hard insoluble objects a life accretes and a
rendering cannot melt, all of it set into the congealed grease like flies in amber, sorted
by nothing, simply settled where it stopped being worth the cost of recovering. The surface
does not move. Nothing tends this place. It is the one corner of the Works where the patient
machinery has admitted defeat and walked away, and the quiet of that admission sits on the
cold trap like a lid.

The trap is the Works' honest confession: that for all the talk of refining, of product,
of nothing wasted, there is always a remainder, always a part that won't reduce, and that
the part that won't reduce is the part that was a person, and that hell throws it in a trap
and forgets it.

<<if not $remainderKnown>>
In the grey-white geology of the float, one shape is still a person — the part of someone the process
could not drive an oil out of, settled here and forgotten, and still, faintly, able to speak.
<<linkreplace "Find the shape in the float that is still a person">><<nobr>><<set $remainderKnown to true>>
//There's always a remainder,// it says, from the cold scum. //That's the thing they don't put on the
brochure. They'll render you down — tallow, candle, soap, the lot — and there's always a part that won't
//go,// that won't reduce, that stays itself in the trap when all the rest's been sold. And do you know
what that part //is?// It's the part that was a //person.// The unrenderable bit. The bit that was
//you.//// A long, thinning pause. //That's why the mark in the master vat never boiled down either, they
say. Same kind of thing as me. Older than rendering. Can't be taken in.// Quieter still. //Remember me.
That's all. Just — remember there was a me.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The shape in the float is a little more reduced than last time, its voice thinner, and it asks nothing of
you now but the thing it asked before: that there was, once, a me, and that you remember it.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward the runoff sluice">><<goto "TheRunoff">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the soap-vat">><<goto "TheSoapVat">><</link>>


:: TheForemansWalk [hell]
A catwalk runs out from the foreman's office over the whole floor — the management's view
of the process, from which the killing-floor resolves into what it really is: not chaos,
not cruelty, but *order*, a clean and rational system for the conversion of the damned into
the goods that make hell comfortable, no malice in it anywhere, only efficiency, only the
quiet pride of a thing well-organised.

From up here you can see all of it at once, and that is what does the damage. The chains
draw themselves into a single legible circuit; the vats line up in their ranks; the line
runs true from the head where the whole arrives to the tail where the product leaves, and
between them every Gorger stands at the one station that is its whole existence, and the
work flows between them without a wasted motion or a raised voice. It looks, from the rail,
like a thing you could be proud to have built. It looks like the answer to a problem stated
cleanly and solved well. You can feel the part of you that recognizes good work wanting,
horribly, to admire it.

It is the most frightening view in hell, because it is the least infernal. It is just a
factory. It just makes what it makes out of what it's given, and what it's given is you.

<<if not $tallykeepPaid>>
A clerk-Gorger walks the catwalk rail, tallying the floor below into a ledger — not the foreman in its
office, but the hand that carries the figures down to it. It will talk, it lets you understand, to a man
who stands it the courtesy of buying into the books.
<<if $coins gte 2>>
<<link "Let it read you into the ledger — a couple of coin">>
<<set $coins to $coins - 2>><<set $tallykeepPaid to true>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>>
<<goto "TheForemansWalk">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
It waits, pleasant and patient, until it is plain you have nothing to enter against your name — and then
turns back to its tally. Come back with a coin or two, and it will read you in.
<</if>>
<<else>>
The Tallykeep folds its delicate hands on the ledger, friendly now, and waits for the question.
<<linkreplace "Ask where the candles go">><<nobr>>
//Up,// it says. //The good tallow's light: candles to the Shambles, the glow of every hearth in hell,
the tune the not-children sing. You'll have walked past your own portion, lit, and never known it.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<linkreplace "Ask about the soap">><<nobr>>
//The cleverest line in the books,// it says, almost admiring. //We sell it //back.// To the
freshly-taken, the ones who came down wanting to stay clean — we render them, press them, stamp a
wholesome word on them, and sell them their own soap. Demand never drops. That's good business, that.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<linkreplace "Ask where the remainder ends up">><<nobr>>
//The trap, then the runoff,// it says, //and the runoff drains to the bottom — the sulphur, the floor of
everything. Nothing's truly wasted down here. It's only ever //relocated.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<set _rg to setup.regardOf(passage())>><<if _rg and _rg.line>>
<<linkreplace "Ask what it sees when it weighs you">><<nobr>>
It runs its eye down you, pricing. //<<= _rg.line>>//<<if not $tallykeepReturned>> And then, gently, it
enters a figure against your name in the ledger, and does not let you read what.<<set $tallykeepReturned to true>><</if>>
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<</if>>
<</if>>

<<link "Toward the boil-house, down the catwalk stair">><<goto "TheBoilHouse">><</link>>
<<link "Out along the conveyor, toward a cold-store off the line">><<goto "TheConveyorFork">><</link>>
<<link "To a duct-control station, off the catwalk">><<goto "TheVentControl">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the foreman's office">><<goto "TheTally">><</link>>


:: TheTallowFalls [hell]
Where the boil-house overflows, a fall of molten tallow pours from a high lip down a stepped
cascade of cooling stone, white at the top where it's still near-boiling, going cream and
then candle-yellow as it cools down the steps, beautiful in the way the worst things down
here are beautiful, a frozen-motion sculpture in rendered fat that rebuilds itself a drip at
a time forever.

Closer, the falls are not one thing but a thousand. Each step has caught its own season of
the cooling — a lip of still-running white, a shelf gone to set cream, a low terrace where
the candle-yellow has hardened into drapery and stalactite and the slow accreted shapes of
a cave that grew in a single afternoon. It is the prettiest thing you have found in hell. It
is made entirely of people. The two facts sit in you side by side and refuse to cancel, and
that refusal is the falls' whole quiet argument: that the rendered and the beautiful were
never opposites here, only stages of the same descent.

You could warm your hands at the top of it. You could lose them. The line between the two is
exactly the width of the line between everything down here: a step, a degree, a small wrong
lean toward the warmth.

<<link "Back to the bank of hooks">><<goto "TheHooks">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the boil-house">><<goto "TheBoilHouse">><</link>>


/* ---------- CLEAN-VS-DIRTY FORK: the conveyor (switch elsewhere) — owner 2026-06-07 ----------
   Two ways to the cold-store: the GUARDED gantry (a gorger foreman holds it — fight OR pay) or the
   DISCREET conveyor crossing — safe ONLY if you've thrown the duct-vent lever at TheVentControl off
   the catwalk (which carries the rendering steam away); cross with the steam still live and it cooks
   into you. The switch is in a different room — go throw it first. Loops back. */

:: TheConveyorFork [hell]
<<nobr>>
The conveyor runs out across the floor toward a cold-store stacked with set tallow and, among it,
unrendered oddments worth taking. Above the belt a gantry crosses dry and clear — but a gorger has
set itself up there as a kind of foreman, and won't have you on its gantry for free. The other way
is the belt itself, low through the rendering-steam that jets across it from the boil below: walkable,
if the steam weren't live. There's a duct-vent control back off the catwalk that would carry the
steam away — <<if $ductVented>>and you've thrown it; the belt runs cool and clear now.<<else>>but you
haven't touched it, and the steam still renders everything that crosses.<</if>>
<</nobr>>

<<if $coldStoreLooted>>
You've been through; the gorger's gone back to the line, the cold-store stands open and emptied.
<<link "Across to the cold-store">><<goto "TheColdStore">><</link>>
<<else>>
//The price of each way is clear from here: the gorger-foreman wants a real fight, or ''22 coins'' for the gantry; and the belt would render ''about 18 filth'' of live steam into your torso<<if $ductVented>> — but you've vented it, and it runs cool now<<else>> — unless you throw the duct-vent lever, back off the catwalk<</if>>.//

<<scentof "gorger">>
<<link "The guarded way — pull the gorger off its gantry and cross dry">>
  <<run setup.startFight("gorger", "TheColdStore")>><<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<if $coins gte 22>>
<<link "The guarded way — pay the gorger-foreman its 22 coins, cross the gantry">>
  <<set $coins to $coins - 22>><<goto "TheColdStore">>
<</link>>
<<else>>(The gorger wants 22 coins for the gantry; you are short.)<</if>>
<<if $ductVented>>
<<link "The discreet way — walk the belt (the steam's vented, it runs cool)">>
  <<goto "TheColdStore">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
<<set _tlbl to "The discreet way — cross the belt through the live steam (" + setup.tollTag("torso", "filth", 18) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "torso" "filth" 18>>
  <<goto "TheColdStore">>
<</link>>
<</if>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back along the catwalk">><<goto "TheForemansWalk">><</link>>


:: TheVentControl [hell]
<<nobr>>
A control station off the catwalk: a panel of greased levers and a single great duct-vent wheel,
labelled in the works' patient hand. Throw it and the rendering-steam that crosses the conveyor down
the line gets carried off up the flue instead — for the floor's sake, not yours, but it works the
same either way.
<</nobr>>

<<if $ductVented>>
The duct-vent is already open; you can hear the steam going up the flue where it used to cross the belt.
<<else>>
<<link "Throw the duct-vent lever">>
  <<set $ductVented to true>>
  The wheel fights you, then gives, and somewhere down the line the steam stops jetting sideways and
  goes where it's told. The conveyor crossing will be cool now.
<</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back along the catwalk">><<goto "TheForemansWalk">><</link>>


:: TheColdStore [hell]
<<nobr>>
The cold-store is a still, cool room stacked with set tallow in grey blocks, and among the blocks the
oddments the line never got around to rendering — including a little coin, pocketed by some hand the
works has long since used up.

The cold here is the deepest you've felt in hell, and the quietest. The blocks are stacked
to the roof in dim grey aisles, each one true-cornered and stamped, the breath of the boil
finally gone out of the air so that for once you can hear your own movement — your feet, your
heart, the small sounds of a thing still running. The oddments turned up between the stacks
are the litter of a hundred such hands: a buckle, a button, a coin gone green at the edge,
the indigestible bits the process passed over and nobody came back for, because the works
only ever comes back for what pays.
<</nobr>>

<<if not $coldStoreLooted>>
<<set $coldStoreLooted to true>>
<<set _g to setup.gainCoins(random(14, 20))>>
You work <<= _g>> cold coin<<if _g neq 1>>s<</if>> out from between the tallow blocks.
<<else>>
The store is stripped now; only the grey blocks remain.
<</if>>

<<link "Back along the catwalk">><<goto "TheForemansWalk">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-8 BAD END ---------- */

:: EndingRendered [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("died")>>
You climb down into the warm, because the warm smells like the only kind thing in hell and
because you are so tired of being raw, of being the one unprocessed thing on a floor that
processes everything, of the work it takes to keep arguing that you are not yet meat. You
lower yourself into the rich dark broth at the bottom of the vat and it closes over you
like a held breath, like a homecoming, like the inside of something that was always going
to have you, and the last of your arguing goes out of you in a long warm sigh.

The vat fills, in time, and boils, in time, and the Works do with you what the Works do,
without malice, with great efficiency, and somewhere up in the Shambles a not-child sings a
jingle for a brand of lamp-oil, and a demon lights a candle in a warm hell parlour, and the
light is kind, and the light is you.

//You stopped being raw. The Works waste nothing, and now they have not wasted you.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>
